==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*sent-|id=feel}}
From {{der|en|it|risentimento}}, or {{der|en|fr|ressentiment}} from an archaic usage of the verb {{m|fr|ressentir}} via {{der|en|fro|sentir}}, from {{der|en|la|[[sentiō]], [[sentīre]]|t=to feel}}. {{doublet|en|resentiment|ressentiment}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|rĭ-zĕntʹmənt}}, {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈzɛnt.mənt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-resentment.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{hyph|en|re|sent|ment}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# [[anger|Anger]] or [[displeasure]] stemming from belief that one has been wronged or betrayed by others; [[indignation]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=08/05/2012|author=Nathan Rabin| title=TV: Review: THE SIMPSONS (CLASSIC): “I Love Lisa” (season 4, episode 15; originally aired 02/11/1993)| url=http://www.avclub.com/articles/i-love-lisa,83183/| journal=AV Club| passage=“I Love Lisa” opens with one of my favorite underappreciated running jokes from ''The Simpsons'': the passive-aggressive, quietly contentious relationship of radio jocks Bill and Marty, whose mindless happy talk regularly gives way to charged exchanges that betray the simmering '''resentment''' and disappointment perpetually lingering just under the surface of their relationship.}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Pride and Prejudice|1|[[wikisource:Pride_and_Prejudice/Chapter_3|3]]|text=Amongst the most violent against him was Mrs. Bennet, whose dislike of his general behaviour was sharpened into particular '''resentment''' by his having slighted one of her daughters.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The state of holding something in the mind as a subject of [[contemplation]], or of being inclined to [[reflect]] upon it; [[feeling]]; [[impression]].
#* {{RQ:More Divine Dialogues|text=He retains so vivid '''resentments''' of the more solid morality.}}
#* {{RQ:Taylor Eniautos|passage={{quote|en|It is a greater wonder that so many of them die, with so little '''resentment''' of their danger.}}}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[satisfaction]]; [[gratitude]]
#* {{quote-book|en|1651||The Council Book|text=The Council taking notice of the many good services performed by Mr. John Milton {{...}} have thought fit to declare their '''resentment''' and good acceptance of the same.}}

